.TH MODCHK 1
.SH NAME
modchk \- check that the kernel's builtin modules answer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B modchk
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Modchk
loads each module built into the kernel and calls through it for an
answer that is known, printing the module, the call, and whether it
matched.  It is meant for a kernel that has been changed under the
modules rather than for the modules themselves.
.PP
.IR Math ,
.IR Keyring ,
.I Crypt
and
.I IPints
are covered:
.B sqrt
and
.BR pow ;
.B sha1
and
.B md5
of
.BR abc ;
.B sha256
of
.BR abc ;
.B strtoip
and
.BR expmod .
.I Sys
is not, because nothing runs without it.
.PP
A module the kernel is not configured with is reported absent and
counted apart from the checks that ran, so that a run in which nothing
could be checked does not read like a run in which everything passed.
The last lines are a count and, only if at least one check ran and none
failed,
.BR MODCHK-OK .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/modchk.b
.SH HISTORY
Written for a change to how a builtin module's exported functions are
named.
.IR Linkm ()
in
.B /libinterp/link.c
finds an exported function by
.B strcmp
against the
.B Link
array that
.IR builtinmod ()
built from the module's
.BR Runtab .
Those names used to be copied into the kernel pool, one small block
each; for a module built into the kernel the
.B Runtab
is a table of string literals in the image, so the copy was 311 blocks
of 64 bytes on an
.B mps2
kernel carrying
.IR Sys ,
.IR Keyring ,
.IR Crypt ,
.I Math
and
.IR IPints .
The names are now used where they lie and that comparison reads kernel
text.  Every Dis program exercises
.I Sys
and
.IR fptest (1)
exercises
.IR Math ,
but nothing on a small board loads the other three.
.PP
.I IPints
is the reason this exists rather than a note in a commit: it is
registered from both
.I Keyring
and
.IR Crypt ,
each in case only the other is configured, and the guard that made
.IR ipintsmodinit ()
idempotent covered the type it creates and not the
.IR builtinmod ()
call under it, so a kernel with both carried two of it.  That guard now
covers both, and this is what says the remaining one still works.
.PP
Run on
.B mps2
under
.IR qemu (1)
and under the host
.IR emu (1),
which is the other Dis word size and another compiler: seven checks,
none failed, no module absent.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR fptest (1),
.IR keyring (2),
.IR crypt (2),
.IR ipints (2)
